---
/**
 * How to report an error.
 *
 * No form, because the site has no backend and no client JavaScript, and a
 * contact form is the one thing that would need both. Reports come by email to
 * an address that exists only for this purpose: it is a Cloudflare Email Routing
 * alias on our own domain, not a personal mailbox, which is the reason it can
 * appear on a public page at all.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const CONTACT = "corrections@aisafetytracker.org";
---

<Base
  title="Corrections"
  description="How to report an error on this site, how reports are handled, and the list of corrections made."
  cites={[]}
>
  <section class="section section--open">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="masthead">
        <h1>Corrections</h1>
        <p class="masthead__meta mono">Reported, fixed, and listed here</p>
      </div>

      <div class="doc">
        <p class="lede">
          Errors on this site can be reported by email. Corrections that change what a figure says
          are listed on this page with the date.
        </p>

        <h2>Reporting an error</h2>
        <p>
          Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>. This address is used only for
          corrections.
        </p>
        <p>
          Include the page, the figure concerned and the value believed to be correct. Where the
          error originates in an upstream dataset, note that, as the correction route differs.
        </p>

        <h2>Handling</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Processing errors</strong> are corrected in the pipeline. The corrected figure
            appears at the next daily refresh. Where the correction changes what a figure says, it
            is listed below with the date.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Upstream errors</strong> are recorded in that source's caveats on the
            <a href="/sources/">sources page</a>, which is where the marker on every affected
            figure points, and reported to the publisher where a route exists.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Disputed judgements</strong>, such as the coding of a jurisdiction in the
            Governance Readiness Index, are reviewed on the same basis. The coding rules and the
            instruments behind each score are published in the
            <a href="/methodology/governance-readiness/">methodology</a>.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Rights holders</h2>
        <p>
          Requests for removal may be sent to the address above. Every source is declared in a
          single registry and every record carries its identifier, so removing a source removes
          everything derived from it.
        </p>

        <h2>Corrections made</h2>
        <p>
          None to date. Corrections that change what a figure says will be listed here with the
          date and the change.
        </p>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
